= i
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

**False.** A connected graph has an <Eulerian cycle> exactly when every vertex has even degree. In $K_n$ every degree is $n-1$, so it has an <Eulerian cycle> precisely when $n$ is odd. The <complete graph> $K_4$ is a counterexample to the statement for all $n\geq3$.

= ii
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

**True.** Label the vertices $1,\ldots,n$. The cycle $1,2,\ldots,n,1$ uses existing edges of the <complete graph> and visits every vertex once before returning. It is a <Hamiltonian cycle> for every $n\geq3$.

= iii
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

**True.** Draw two nested squares and join corresponding corners. This is the <hypercube graph>: each square gives one four-cycle and the four connecting edges give the remaining coordinate direction. The edges have no crossings, furnishing a <plane graph>.

\Image[2007-planar-cube.png]
{title=Two nested squares give a crossing-free embedding of the <hypercube graph>}

= iv
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}
{c}

**False.** The cube has eight vertices and twelve edges. <Euler formula for a connected planar graph> for its connected <plane graph> gives $8-12+F=2$, so
$$
\boxed{F=6.}
$$
The nested-square embedding has four faces between the squares, one inside the smaller square and the unbounded exterior face.

= v
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}
{c}

**True.** Represent a vertex of $Q_4$ by a four-bit string, with an edge changing exactly one bit. Color according to the parity of the sum of the bits. Every edge joins opposite parities, giving a proper two-coloring. Since the graph has edges, one color is impossible, so $\chi(Q_4)=2$.

= vi
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=vi}
{c}

**False.** In $K_{r,s}$ the $r$ vertices in one class have degree $s$, while the $s$ vertices in the other have degree $r$. For positive $r,s$, connectedness is automatic, and Euler's criterion requires both $r$ and $s$ to be even. Evenness of their sum alone allows both to be odd. For example $K_{1,3}$ has $r+s=4$ but all four degrees are odd, so it has no <Eulerian cycle>.

= vii
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=vii}
{c}

**False.** The four-cycle $C_4=K_{2,2}$ is bipartite, with alternating color classes, but contains a cycle and therefore is not a <tree (graph theory)>.

= viii
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=viii}
{c}

**True.** Choose a root of the <tree (graph theory)> and color every vertex according to the parity of its graph distance from that root. The unique-path property implies that the endpoints of each edge have distances differing by one. Thus every edge crosses the two color classes, proving <bipartite graph>.

= ix
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ix}
{c}

**False.** In a <Prüfer code>, the degree of a label is one plus its number of occurrences. Here labels $1,\ldots,n-2$ occur once and labels $n-1,n$ do not occur. Thus the former have degree two and the latter degree one. The decoded <tree (graph theory)> is the path
$$
(n-1),1,2,\ldots,n-2,n
$$
with the evident single-edge interpretation when $n=2$. Independently, no vertex of a simple <tree (graph theory)> on $n$ vertices can have degree $n$ at all. The degree claim in the statement is therefore impossible.

= x
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=x}
{c}

**True.** Each <connected component> of a <forest> is a <tree (graph theory)>. If the component sizes are $n_1,\ldots,n_k$, their edge counts are $n_1-1,\ldots,n_k-1$, including isolated vertices as size-one components. Summing gives $m=n-k$, hence
$$
\boxed{k=n-m.}
$$

= S
{parent=2007}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Combinatorial Optimisation}

= 1
{parent=2007/S}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=1}
{c}

The first four jobs all have deadlines at most ten, but their total processing time is $2+4+3+2=11$. They cannot all be completed by time ten on one machine, so at least one is late in every schedule.

<Moore's algorithm> processes jobs in nondecreasing deadline order. Whenever the <electric current> on-time list first misses the new job's deadline, remove from that list a job with the largest processing time and put it on the late list. Continue, then append the rejected jobs after the on-time list. The theorem for this algorithm states that it minimizes the number of late jobs when all jobs are initially available.

The source order is already deadline order. The steps are
$$
\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}
\text{job added}&\text{tentative completion}&\text{action}&
\text{on-time list afterward}\\\hline
1&2\leq3&\text{keep}&1\\
2&6\leq7&\text{keep}&1,2\\
3&9\leq9&\text{keep}&1,2,3\\
4&11>10&\text{remove }2\ (p_2=4)&1,3,4\\
5&12\leq13&\text{keep}&1,3,4,5\\
6&16>15&\text{remove }5\ (p_5=5)&1,3,4,6
\end{array}
$$
After the first removal the cumulative time is seven; after the second it is eleven. Append jobs two and five. One optimal schedule is
$$
\boxed{1,3,4,6,2,5,\qquad\text{two late jobs}.}
$$
Its completion times are $2,5,7,11,15,20$. Comparing with the corresponding deadlines verifies that precisely jobs two and five are late. Moore's theorem certifies that no schedule can improve on that number.

= 2
{parent=2007/S}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}
{c}

<flow conservation> determines the unspecified initial arcs:
$$
(x_{12},x_{13},x_{24},x_{34},x_{35},x_{46},x_{56})
=(1,3,1,2,1,3,1).
$$
Every entry respects capacity, and the initial value is four. In the <residual network> take the <augmenting path>
$$
1\longrightarrow2\longrightarrow4
\longrightarrow3\longrightarrow5\longrightarrow6.
$$
The step $4\to3$ is the reverse residual arc of the used arc $3\to4$. Its use cancels some existing flow rather than sending new physical flow on a nonexistent original arc. The residual capacities along the path are $2,1,2,2,1$, so augment by their minimum, one.

The resulting original-arc flows are
$$
\boxed{(x_{12},x_{13},x_{24},x_{34},x_{35},x_{46},x_{56})
=(2,3,2,1,2,3,2).}
$$
Their value is five. The cut separating the sink from all other nodes has capacity $c_{46}+c_{56}=3+2=5$. Every feasible flow is bounded by this cut capacity, so the value-five flow is optimal.

\Image[2007-residual-flow.png]
{title=One unit of residual rerouting cancels flow on 34 and raises the total from four to five}

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}
{c}

Start the augmenting-path algorithm from the zero flow. With integer capacities and an integer <electric current> flow, every residual capacity is an integer, so a path bottleneck is a positive integer. Augmenting by it preserves integrality and increases the flow value by at least one. The value is bounded by the finite total capacity leaving the source, so the process terminates. At termination there is no <augmenting path>, hence the flow is maximum by the <max-flow min-cut theorem>. Its value is an integer.

Equivalently a minimum cut has capacity equal to the sum of integer arc capacities, so the <maximum flow> value equals an integer. Thus **integer capacities guarantee an integer optimal volume**, even though arbitrary feasible real-valued flows need not themselves have integer entries.

= T
{parent=2007}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Linear Programming}

= 1
{parent=2007/T}
{scope}

= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}
{c}

The dual is
$$
\boxed{\text{minimize }b^Ty
\quad\text{subject to }A^Ty\geq c,\quad y\geq0.}
$$
<weak duality> says that any feasible primal $x$ and dual $y$ satisfy $c^Tx\leq b^Ty$. Indeed,
$$
c^Tx\leq(A^Ty)^Tx=y^TAx\leq y^Tb.
$$
The first inequality uses $x\geq0$ and $A^Ty-c\geq0$, and the second uses $y\geq0$ and $b-Ax\geq0$. This proves the theorem. In particular, feasible solutions with equal <objective function> values are optimal for both problems.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}
{c}

With primal constraints $Ax=b$, the dual variable is unrestricted in sign:
$$
\boxed{\text{minimize }b^Ty
\quad\text{subject to }A^Ty\geq c,\quad y\in\mathbb R^m.}
$$
Membership of $x$ in the <nonnegative orthant> still yields $c^Tx\leq y^TAx$, but the next step is now the equality $y^TAx=y^Tb$, so no sign condition on $y$ is needed. Equivalently an equality constraint can be replaced by two opposite inequalities whose nonnegative multipliers combine into one free multiplier.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}
{c}

The two primal constraints are equalities, so let the two dual variables $u,v$ be free. Primal feasibility restricts $x$ to the <nonnegative orthant>, giving
$$
\text{minimize }300(u+v),\qquad
\begin{cases}
2u+8v\geq4,\\
3u+v\geq2,\\
4u+v\geq3,\\
2u+5v\geq5.
\end{cases}
$$
Thus the <feasible region> lies above all four lines:
$$
v\geq\max\{1/2-u/4,\ 2-3u,\ 3-4u,\ 1-2u/5\}.
$$
Its lower boundary follows the third line up to $(5/9,7/9)$, the fourth line from there to $(10/3,-1/3)$, and then the first line. The second line never determines the envelope. Along these three pieces $u+v$ respectively has slopes $-3$, $3/5$, and $3/4$, so its unique minimum is at the first corner:
$$
\boxed{u=\frac59,\qquad v=\frac79,\qquad
300(u+v)=400.}
$$

At this dual point the first two inequalities are strict, while the last two are equalities. <complementary slackness> therefore suggests $x_1=x_2=0$. The remaining primal equations become $4x_3+2x_4=300$, $x_3+5x_4=300$, giving $x_3=x_4=50$. This is nonnegative and satisfies both equations. Its <objective function> is $3\cdot50+5\cdot50=400$, equal to the feasible dual value. <weak duality> proves
$$
\boxed{x^*=(0,0,50,50),\qquad z^*=400.}
$$
No nonnegativity restriction was imposed on the dual variables; the negative-$v$ second corner is part of the feasible boundary, even though it is not optimal.

\Image[2007-dual-envelope.png]
{title=The dual lower envelope has its <objective function> minimum at the intersection of the third and fourth constraints}
